1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,737
Welcome, everybody, to the lovely house and gardens of Rosewood,

2
00:00:04,737 --> 00:00:09,000
once the home of the famous writer, Sebastian George.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,000
He bought the house in 1902 although he had first seen it two years earlier.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,040
At that time the owners let it out to a tenant

5
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:21,620
because George was too slow making up his mind to buy it.

6
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:23,620
When it came back on the market

7
00:00:23,620 --> 00:00:28,563
there was no hesitation and he bought it immediately,

8
00:00:28,563 --> 00:00:32,180
for £9,300, even though the house had no bathroom

9
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:35,940
no running water upstairs, and no electricity.

10
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:40,320
When he came here, he'd been married for ten years.

11
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:45,810
During that time, he'd become one of the most famous writers in the English-speaking world.

12
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:51,910
His professional success was enormous, but his personal life wasn't as successful.

13
00:00:51,910 --> 00:00:54,430
He was no longer on speaking terms

14
00:00:54,430 --> 00:01:01,630
with his brother and had been devastated by the death at the age of seven of his elder daughter, Josephine.

15
00:01:01,630 --> 00:01:06,590
Moving to Rosewood allowed the family to start a new life.

16
00:01:06,590 --> 00:01:12,658
George regarded Rosewood as a pure example of a traditional country house of this part of England

17
00:01:12,658 --> 00:01:15,870
and did some of his most successful writing here.

18
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:20,660
The house and its grounds became the family haven

19
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:23,500
and their escape to privacy and quiet.

20
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:28,100
The walls, and the mullioned windows were built of the local sandstone,

21
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:30,260
the tiles on the roofs

22
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:34,617
and the bricks of the chimney stack were baked from local clay,

23
00:01:34,617 --> 00:01:39,600
and the wooden structures inside came from oak trees which grow around here.

24
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:45,080
Now, please look at the map I've given you of the house and gardens.

25
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:48,650
We're here at the Information Centre.

26
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:51,840
Follow the path marked with the arrow

27
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:56,500
and the first area you come to is the orchard on your left.

28
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:59,131
As you go further down the path,

29
00:01:59,131 --> 00:02:01,640
there's the kitchen garden on the right

30
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:06,273
and as you go round the first sharp corner you will find,

31
00:02:06,273 --> 00:02:07,280
to your left, an area

32
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:13,791
where different types of pear tree have been planted as well as some lovely flowers,

33
00:02:13,791 --> 00:02:17,550
and this is known as Pear Alley-designed by George himself.

34
00:02:17,550 --> 00:02:24,290
Next to this is the greenhouse where some exotic plants and fruits are grown.

35
00:02:24,290 --> 00:02:27,145
Follow the path round the second corner

36
00:02:27,145 --> 00:02:34,080
and on your right you will see the entrance to the Mulberry Garden with its 500-year-old tree.

37
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:40,310
Past the Mulberry Garden, follow the path until you reach the front of the house.

38
00:02:40,310 --> 00:02:45,173
I suggest you spend a good hour wandering around this lovely building.

39
00:02:45,173 --> 00:02:48,820
A guide takes visitor groups round every two hours.

40
00:02:48,820 --> 00:02:53,723
If you would like to purchase any of George's books or other souvenirs,

41
00:02:53,723 --> 00:02:58,200
then leave the house by the side entrance where you will find our shop

42
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:01,266
which is situated between the house

43
00:03:01,266 --> 00:03:05,300
and the garage which contains the magnificent old Rolls-Royce car

44
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:07,300
which used to belong to George.

45
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:13,430
I expect by this time you may also be in need of a rest and some refreshment.

46
00:03:13,430 --> 00:03:19,880
Most visitors are, so why don't you visit the tea room on the far side of the garage?

47
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:26,090
If you have time, there is a lovely walk down towards the River Dudwell.

48
00:03:26,090 --> 00:03:29,430
For me, this is the best part of the estate.

49
00:03:29,430 --> 00:03:34,290
This isn't on the map but it is all clearly signposted.

50
00:03:34,290 --> 00:03:38,310
You cross the field which spreads along the banks of the river.

51
00:03:38,310 --> 00:03:41,890
In spring, this area is well worth a visit.

52
00:03:41,890 --> 00:03:47,527
Spend a minute or two watching the water pass by underneath as you cross the footbridge,

53
00:03:47,527 --> 00:03:51,050
and then continue along the River Walk through the woodland.

54
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:56,320
On a hot summer's day the trees along this path provide welcome shade.

55
00:03:56,320 --> 00:03:59,163
Eventually you come to the water mill

56
00:03:59,163 --> 00:04:05,660
which used to provide the electricity for the house-only about four hours every evening-in George's time.

57
00:04:05,660 --> 00:04:07,697
And, finally, for those of you

58
00:04:07,697 --> 00:04:12,100
who would like to see stunning views of the surrounding countryside

59
00:04:12,100 --> 00:04:14,149
and who are a little bit more energetic,

60
00:04:14,149 --> 00:04:18,560
when you return from the mill take the first turning on your left

61
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:20,600
and climb up to the viewpoint.

62
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:22,000
You won't regret it.

63
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:23,790
Enjoy your visit!
